.TH FROST 1 "" "frost 0.10.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
frost \- a readable shell scripting language that is auditable before it runs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B frost
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISCRIPT\fR
.br
.B frost
.I init
[\fIFOLDER\fR]
.br
.B frost
.I diff
\fIBEFORE\fR \fIAFTER\fR
.br
.B frost
.I mcp
|
.I context
|
.I keystore
.SH DESCRIPTION
frost runs shell scripts that can be read before they are run. A command is
argv and never a string, so a value can never become syntax: there is no
.BR eval ,
no command substitution and no string interpolation to represent.
.PP
Because every effect is a node in the tree, frost can print a capability
manifest before a single process is spawned, and a policy can refuse a script
that does more than it was meant to.
.PP
\fISCRIPT\fR may be a file, a folder whose \fB.frost\fR files are all
reviewed, or \fB\-\fR to read one script from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
show this help message and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
show program's version number and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-try\fR
open a scratchpad for trying chunk expressions
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR
print the script in canonical layout
.TP
\fB\-\-write\fR
with \-\-format, rewrite the file in place
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
parse only; do not run
.TP
\fB\-\-ast\fR
parse and dump the syntax tree
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR
print each statement as it runs
.TP
\fB\-\-run\-id\fR \fIID\fR
identity for this execution; otherwise FROST_RUN_ID, otherwise generated
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\-to\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
write the trace to a file instead of standard error
.TP
\fB\-\-explain\fR
describe what the script can do, without running it
.TP
\fB\-\-sarif\fR
findings as SARIF, for code scanning on a pull request
.TP
\fB\-\-exit\-codes\fR
what each exit status means
.TP
\fB\-\-completion\fR \fISHELL\fR
print a completion script for bash or zsh
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
emit the result as JSON: the manifest with \-\-explain, and otherwise the diagnostics, with a repair for each one that has a mechanical fix
.TP
\fB\-\-repair\fR
apply every high\-confidence repair and print the result; refuses to write unless it then parses
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIFILE\fR
check the script against a policy file, then run it only if it passes
.TP
\fB\-\-watch\fR
re\-run the review whenever the script changes, until interrupted
.TP
\fB\-\-brief\fR
one line per script: what it touches and the verdict, for a log with forty of them in it
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-policy\fR
ignore a frost.policy found beside the script
.TP
\fB\-\-keystore\fR \fIFILE\fR
the keystore that 'the secret ...' reads from
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fIROLE\fR
the role this run acts as; decides which secrets it may read
.TP
\fB\-\-lock\fR
record the sha256 of every module in <script>.lock
.TP
\fB\-\-frozen\fR
refuse to run if any module differs from the lockfile
.TP
\fB\-\-approve\fR
record what the script may do, in <script>.approved
.TP
\fB\-\-as\-approved\fR
insist on an approval: refuse if there is none, or if the script gained a capability since it
.TP
\fB\-\-against\fR \fIFILE\fR
compare the manifest with a recorded approval, without running anything
.TP
\fB\-\-policy\-from\fR
write a starter policy describing what this script already does
.TP
\fB\-\-sign\-with\fR \fIKEYFILE\fR
sign the approval with this key
.TP
\fB\-\-approver\fR \fINAME\fR
who is approving; recorded inside the signature
.TP
\fB\-\-commit\fR \fISHA\fR
the revision this approval was read against
.TP
\fB\-\-new\-approver\-key\fR \fIKEYFILE\fR
write a new signing key and print its public half
.TP
\fB\-\-events\fR \fIFILE\fR
write one JSON object per event, for Splunk, New Relic and anything else that reads NDJSON; \- for standard error
.TP
\fB\-\-events\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
ndjson (default, streamed) or otel (OTLP/JSON traces, written when the run ends)
.TP
\fB\-\-egress\-rules\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
write the host allow\-list as configuration for the proxy that can enforce it
.TP
\fB\-\-deadline\fR \fISECONDS\fR
stop the whole run after this long, running any cleanup on the way out
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
with \-\-check, exit 1 when the verdict is dangerous rather than only reporting it
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-output\fR \fISIZE\fR
the most a run may return from its commands, as '10MB' or '500kb'; the child is killed at the ceiling rather than measured after the fact
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-one\-command\fR \fISIZE\fR
the most any single command may return
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-written\fR \fISIZE\fR
the most a run may write to files
.TP
\fB\-\-enforce\-hosts\fR
check a command's real destination before it runs, so a computed URL can be allowed rather than only refused
.TP
\fB\-\-automated\fR
this run is unattended: refuse anything that would widen what a script may do
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-approval\fR
run even though <script>.approved says otherwise
.TP
\fB\-\-record\fR \fIFILE\fR
run the script and write down everything it did
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\fR \fIFILE\fR
run the script against a recording, spawning nothing and changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-sandbox\fR
hold the boundary the policy declares; refuses to run if it cannot be enforced here
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the script ran and finished
.TP
.B 1
a command failed, or the script hit a runtime error
.TP
.B 2
it did not parse, or frost was asked for something it could not set up
.TP
.B 3
a policy, an import ceiling, a sandbox boundary or an approval said no;
nothing ran
.TP
.B 4
a replay did not match its recording
.TP
.B 124
the run passed its deadline
.TP
.B 125
the run passed a volume ceiling
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
disable colour, whatever the terminal says
.TP
.B FROST_SITE_POLICY_DIR
a folder of policies applied to every run, which can only add rules
.TP
.B FROST_AUTOMATED
treat the run as unattended, refusing to approve or to draft a policy change
.SH FILES
.TP
.B frost.policy
found beside the script, or above it, and applied unless \fB\-\-no\-policy\fR
.SH SEE ALSO
The full reference is LANGUAGE.md in the distribution, and
